WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.870
HealthSteward is an AI care coordinator.

2
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:11.350
For millions of adults in the U.S., care is scattered across clinics and hospitals, and only the patient sees all of it.

3
00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:19.260
HealthSteward pulls it into one record, then helps you prepare for visits, involve the people you trust, and ask questions about your own record.

4
00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:20.580
Let's meet Emily.

5
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.933
Emily has multiple chronic conditions.

6
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:30.896
She sees four doctors across three health systems, and each condition means recurring labs and imaging.

7
00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:34.250
She is the one holding all of it together.

8
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:40.267
HealthSteward changes that. This is Emily's home page. First, she connects her health systems.

9
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:46.550
Most systems link directly, and for a private practice like Wells, she can upload PDFs instead.

10
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:53.782
Before any data is read, she sees what is shared and for how long, and she can revoke it any time.

11
00:00:53.782 --> 00:00:55.420
Her data is never sold.

12
00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.400
Records are sorted into conditions, medications and labs, with conflicts flagged.

13
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:11.259
Before every visit, Emily has to recall months of symptoms and changes, and decide what is worth raising.

14
00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:15.945
HealthSteward does that groundwork from her records, plus any diary entries she adds.

15
00:01:15.945 --> 00:01:19.910
Identifying details are removed before the AI sees any of it.

16
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:30.937
She then gets what she would otherwise piece together from memory: what changed since her last visit, and questions worth asking.

17
00:01:30.937 --> 00:01:34.690
Every claim is traced to its source and checked against the record.

18
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.700
And she can email it straight to her doctor.

19
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.079
Emily does not have to carry this load alone.

20
00:01:41.079 --> 00:01:45.401
Her Village is the people she trusts, and she decides what each one sees.

21
00:01:45.401 --> 00:01:52.810
She can also choose which of them are emergency contacts, like her husband, Jason, who can answer a provider's questions if she can't speak.

22
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.889
Sometimes Emily might just need a quick answer, like what changed with her thyroid dose.

23
00:01:58.889 --> 00:02:03.200
She asks out loud, and gets a plain-language answer with the documents behind it.

24
00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:13.214
When a provider needs answers in an emergency, her designated emergency contacts, like Jason, can ask questions about her record, within what she allows.

25
00:02:13.214 --> 00:02:20.010
If the answer isn't there, it says so instead of guessing, and Emily is informed about every question asked.

26
00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.388
Emily's story is far from unique.

27
00:02:23.388 --> 00:02:30.450
About half of U.S. adults have multiple chronic conditions, and an estimated one in four of them face fragmented care.

28
00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:35.135
Say we reach just one percent of them in five years.

29
00:02:35.135 --> 00:02:37.873
That is still three hundred forty thousand patients.

30
00:02:37.873 --> 00:02:43.350
At twenty dollars a month each, that is about eighty-two million dollars a year in revenue.

31
00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:50.610
And the need goes beyond chronic illness: a serious diagnosis, caregiving, a search for answers.

32
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.680
HealthSteward: the coordinator your care never had.
